 ## Overview
-Ray marching with single scattering is a real-time technique for rendering volumetric clouds by discretizing the Radiative Transfer Equation (RTE) along view rays and approximating light transport with a single scattering event [S3, unofficial]. It combines procedural noise for density generation with a physically-based lighting model to produce dynamic, three-dimensional cloudscapes [S1, S3, unofficial]. It is distinguished from multi-scattering methods by its computational efficiency, trading physical accuracy for performance, and from billboard-based methods by its ability to render volumetric depth and internal structure [S2, S3, unofficial].
+Ray marching with single scattering is a real-time technique for rendering volumetric clouds by discretizing the Radiative Transfer Equation (RTE) along view rays and approximating light transport with a single scattering event [S3, unofficial]. It combines procedural noise for density generation with a physically-based lighting model to produce dynamic, three-dimensional cloudscapes [S1, S3, unofficial]. It is distinguished from multi-scattering methods by its computational efficiency, trading physical accuracy for performance, and from billboard-based methods by its ability to render volumetric depth and internal structure [S3, unofficial].
 
